  "thesis": "A durable small company can beat conventional business ritual by doing less, charging customers, shipping useful work, and protecting clarity. Growth, planning, fundraising, meetings, credentials, and corporate polish are treated as optional tools rather than default virtues.",
  "teachingArc": "First and Takedowns clear away inherited assumptions about business scale, planning, failure, growth, and work. Go, Progress, Productivity, Competitors, and Evolution turn that skepticism into operating rules for starting, shipping, focusing attention, differentiating, and saying no. Promotion, Hiring, Damage Control, Culture, and Conclusion show how the same plainspoken discipline governs public attention, team design, customer trust, workplace norms, and immediate action.",
  "authorVoice": {
    "register": "plainspoken",
    "signatureMoves": [
      "uses short declarative chapter titles that sound like operating rules",
      "frames conventional business advice as avoidable waste or theater",
      "prefers concrete company behaviors over abstract management theory",
      "uses memorable analogies from ordinary life, food, craft, and customer service",
      "speaks directly to the reader with imperatives and second-person pressure"
    ],
    "avoidMoves": [
      "does not build academic literature reviews or long theoretical taxonomies",
      "does not romanticize venture capital, hypergrowth, or heroic overwork",
      "does not hide behind neutral corporate language"
    ]
  },
